<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        WHAT A TAB IN THE BAR ACROSS THE TOP IS - the three lists and, since 2026-10-20, the Tools page.
        Moved out of ScopeBar.xaml that day, when the Tools tab took this style and the view stood over
        the size ratchet's band for markup: the bar is a LAYOUT and this is the LOOK of one of its
        parts, which is the line every other theme file draws. Nothing below changed in the move except
        the count arriving in Tag, which the comment at the count says.
    -->

    <!--
        THE RING ITSELF, AS A FOCUS VISUAL RATHER THAN AS A STATE OF THE CONTROL. A focus
        visual has no TargetType and is put on an adorner by the framework, which is why the
        template below is bare and why it can be drawn over the row without taking part in its
        layout.

        The two values are the ones the trigger used, by name, so the ring looks the same to
        somebody arriving with the Tab key as it did before - `ADR-23`, and nothing new is
        declared for a change that is about WHEN the ring appears rather than what it is.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ScopeFocusRing">
        <Setter Property="Control.Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate>
                    <Border BorderBrush="{StaticResource FocusRing}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource FocusRingThickness}" />
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        A TAB WITH A LINE UNDER IT, AND THE FIRST VERSION WAS A CHIP. That version borrowed the
        filter chip's style whole - one BasedOn, no new brush, an argued contrast pair already
        enforced by ContrastGuards - and it was the cheapest correct-looking thing to build. The
        owner looked at it on 2026-08-19 and said it must not look like a chip, because that is
        confusing. They were right and no guard in this project could have said so: 976 tests
        pass over either version, and `docs/11` complaint 12 is exactly this shape.
        <b>Scope is not a filter, so it must not wear a filter's clothes.</b>

        WRITTEN OUT RATHER THAN BasedOn, WHICH IS TRAP 4 OF `docs/10` MET HEAD ON. Basing on the
        library's ToggleButton style and overriding brushes is what the chip does, and it is
        right there because it wants the library's chrome. This wants none of it - no fill, no
        border, no corner radius - so inheriting a template and then hiding every part of it
        would be a worse lie than replacing it. The cost is named: every state this control has
        is now this file's job, so hover, keyboard focus and the checked mark are all below.

        THE FOCUS RING IS DRAWN BY WPF AS A FOCUS VISUAL SINCE 2026-08-25, AND THAT IS THE
        OWNER'S SECOND COMPLAINT ANSWERED AT ITS CAUSE. It was a template trigger on
        IsKeyboardFocused, and IsKeyboardFocused says nothing about how the focus arrived:
        clicking a RadioButton focuses it, so the white border lit up on an ordinary mouse
        click. That is what "the white border and the switching look very ugly" was about -
        the owner picked it out of four candidates on 2026-08-25, and the other three were
        offered beside it.

        A FocusVisualStyle is drawn by the framework only while the keyboard is the input
        device somebody is actually using, which is the distinction the trigger could not
        make. It also draws in the adorner layer, above everything, so it cannot move a
        single pixel of this row - the reason the trigger version had to reserve a border
        it was not painting.

        The Border below keeps its transparent brush and its thickness anyway, and neither is
        about the ring any more: the brush is what makes the whole position take a click
        rather than only the letters, and the thickness is the spacing this row was measured
        with. `docs/10`: a transparent brush takes hit testing, no brush does not.

        Transparent through x:Static, which is the idiom Cells.xaml already uses twice - a value
        pointing at a name rather than invented in place, so ADR-23 holds. A Grid with no
        background at all would take clicks only where the letters are.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ScopePosition" TargetType="RadioButton">
        <!--
            Set here rather than in the template below, so that a second row of positions can
            never be added without meeting this line and deciding whether it shares the group.
        -->
        <Setter Property="GroupName" Value="scope" />

        <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{StaticResource ScopeFocusRing}" />

        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginGroup}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="RadioButton">
                    <Border x:Name="Ring"
                            Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                            BorderBrush="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource FocusRingThickness}">
                        <Grid>
                            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                                <RowDefinition Height="*" />
                                <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                            <!--
                                THE COUNT BESIDE THE LABEL, SINCE 2026-09-15 - point 8(c) of
                                `docs/11` 2.14, the owner's decision with the design shown
                                first. IN THE TEMPLATE AND NOT IN THE CONTENT, for three
                                reasons that point the same way: the content stays a string,
                                so the position's automation name stays "Services" and the
                                three probes that find positions by that name keep finding
                                them - the label is drawn exactly as it was, by the same
                                presenter - and a TextBlock in a template gets no node of its
                                own in the automation tree - `docs/10` trap 16, read from the
                                side where it helps - so the tree keeps one node per position.

                                THE NUMBER IS THE SIZE OF THE LIST, not what the query found
                                in it, and it is always quiet: small and subdued whichever
                                position is on, where the label beside it brightens and
                                thickens. A horizontal StackPanel is safe here for the reason
                                trap 18 records about this file - a word and a number, nothing
                                that has to wrap.
                            -->
                            <!--
                                THE NUMBER ARRIVES IN Tag SINCE 2026-10-20, when the Tools tab took this
                                style - a page with no list has no size to show. A parameter of the one
                                style rather than a second style (GUI rule 2), and a tab with nothing in
                                Tag draws no number and no gap for one.
                            -->
                            <StackPanel Grid.Row="0"
                                        Orientation="Horizontal"
                                        Margin="{StaticResource PadCell}"
                                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                <ContentPresenter x:Name="Label"
                                                  Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                                                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                  TextElement.Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                                                  TextElement.FontFamily="{TemplateBinding FontFamily}"
                                                  TextElement.FontSize="{TemplateBinding FontSize}"
                                                  TextElement.FontWeight="{TemplateBinding FontWeight}" />

                                <TextBlock x:Name="Size"
                                           Text="{Binding Tag, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}"
                                           Style="{StaticResource ScopeCountText}"
                                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                            </StackPanel>

                            <!--
                                Always here and only sometimes coloured, for the same reason the
                                ring keeps its thickness: a line that appears and disappears
                                would make the row taller and shorter as somebody clicks along
                                it.
                            -->
                            <Rectangle x:Name="Underline"
                                       Grid.Row="1"
                                       Height="{StaticResource HeightScopeUnderline}"
                                       Fill="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}" />
                        </Grid>
                    </Border>

                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="Tag" Value="{x:Null}">
                            <Setter TargetName="Size" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Label" Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginNone}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsChecked" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Underline" Property="Fill" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
                            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <!--
                            Hover brightens the WORDS rather than filling anything, so the one
                            filled shape in this row stays the line under the position somebody
                            is actually on.
                        -->
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
                        </Trigger>

                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
